# Wick — The assets you produce

Wick ships with no pre-made art or sound. The machine this project is built on
carries asset tools on its `PATH`, and the build produces every sprite, sheet,
icon, and sound the game uses with them, commits the produced files, and wires
them in. This file is the contract: what to produce, which tool produces it,
and how the game consumes it.

Production is a one-time step. The tools are development tools of this
machine; the committed files are the assets, and the build bundles them, so
`npm ci` and `npm run build` invoke no tool and the built site fetches nothing
from outside its own `dist/`. Loading a produced file below states where the
committed files live; their layout under it is yours.

## The tools

| Tool | Produces | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `draw` | a single sprite → one PNG | the still sprites: the idle lamplighter, the icons, the gems, the pickups, the ground tile, and the effects that hold one shape |
| `draw-sheet` | a sprite sheet → one PNG per frame | everything that moves on its own: the walk cycles, the death puff, and the animated effects |
| `sfx-synth` | a procedural sound → `.wav` | the cues |
| `sfx-sample` | a sampled sound over a baked pack → `.wav` | the cues |
| `music` | sequenced music over a baked bank → `.wav` + `.mid` | the music bed |

Each is a command-line tool, and `<tool> --help` states its operations
(`<tool> <operation> --help` for one operation's flags). In outline, each
records the operations you run and then renders the finished file. `draw` and
`draw-sheet` rasterize a fixed-size RGBA canvas from drawing operations;
`draw-sheet` takes a `--frame` on each operation, so a sheet lands as separate
files, one per frame, and it offers layers with keyframes, for a shape painted
once and moved across the frames. The audio tools record voices, layers, or
notes and render a PCM `.wav`. `sfx-sample` draws on a baked sample pack and
`music` on a baked instrument bank, both browsed through their help; `music`
emits a portable `.mid` beside the `.wav` you play, and both are committed.

`particle-2d` is on the `PATH` as well. Wick plays no particle system, and any
flourish beyond the produced sprites is drawn in code.

## The sprites

Every sprite is pixel art drawn at one unit per pixel, so a sprite `24` pixels
wide stands `24` units wide in the world, and the game samples it
nearest-neighbor (`imageSmoothingEnabled = false`) so it stays crisp at the
stage's fit. The sizes below are the intent rather than a tolerance: each thing
reads at its world scale, an enemy filling about its collision circle and the
lamplighter standing a little taller than the enemies around it. Produce
these:

| Asset | Tool | Frames | Sized about |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| lamplighter walk | `draw-sheet` | `4` to `6` | `24 x 32` |
| lamplighter idle | `draw` | `1` | `24 x 32` |
| each of the ten common enemies, a walk cycle | `draw-sheet` | `2` to `4` each | twice its radius in `ENEMIES`, from `16` for a gnat to `36` for a hound |
| mothwing | `draw-sheet` | `2` to `4` | `56 x 56` |
| owl | `draw-sheet` | `2` to `4` | `72 x 72` |
| the Dark | `draw-sheet` | `2` to `4` | `80 x 80` |
| enemy death puff, shared by every enemy | `draw-sheet` | `3` to `4` | `24 x 24` |
| the ten base weapon effects | `draw` / `draw-sheet` | as the table below | matched to the shape it damages |
| the six evolved weapon effects | `draw` / `draw-sheet` | as their base | matched to the shape it damages |
| the sixteen weapon icons, the ten passive icons, and the lamp-oil icon | `draw` | `1` each | `24 x 24` |
| gems: small, medium, large | `draw` | `1` each | `8`, `12`, `16` |
| chest, bread, draft | `draw` | `1` each | `24 x 24` |
| ground tile | `draw` | `1` | `64 x 64` |

Every enemy is told from every other at a glance, by silhouette and not by hue
alone, and the two elites and the Dark read as larger and heavier than the
commons. The three gem tiers are told apart by size and form. The ground tile
sits flush against itself on all four sides, since the ground is that tile
repeated across the world.

### The weapon effects

Each weapon has one effect the game draws wherever the weapon's shape is live,
as `specs/weapons.md` and `specs/evolutions.md` define that shape. Produce each
effect at about its weapon's level-`1` size and scale it in code to the live
shape, which `areaMul` and later levels grow.

| Effect | Weapon | Form | Drawn over |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| slash | Taper | one sprite | the `width x height` rectangle, for `SLASH_FLASH` (`0.1`) seconds |
| bolt | Ember | one sprite | the bolt's circle, for its life |
| dart | Pin | one sprite | the dart's circle, for its life |
| lantern | Lantern | one sprite | each lantern's circle, for its life |
| aura ring | Halo | one sprite, a ring | the aura's radius, always, with its pulse readable |
| puddle | Oil Splash | one sprite | the puddle's circle, for its life |
| strike | Spark | a sheet, played once | the strike's `area`, for `SPARK_FLASH` (`0.2`) seconds |
| shard | Shard | one sprite | the shard's circle, for its life |
| sconce | Sconce | a sheet, spinning | the sconce's circle, for its life |
| flare burst | Flare | a sheet, played once | the burst's radius, for `FLARE_FLASH` (`0.4`) seconds |

Each of the six evolved weapons, Pyre, Beacon, Hail, Chandelier, Corona, and
Blaze, has an effect of the same form as its base's and visibly distinct from
it, so a player sees at a glance that the tool has transformed.

### Animation

A sheet's frames are separate PNG files the game animates, advancing the frame
on a timer of your choosing so the motion reads at world scale. The lamplighter
plays the walk cycle while moving and the idle sprite while still, and faces
the way `facing` says: produce one facing and mirror it in code, or produce
both. Each enemy loops its walk cycle for as long as it lives. The death puff
plays once through at the position an enemy died and is then gone; it is a
picture, and it damages nothing. The strike and the flare burst play once
through over their flash time, and the sconce spins for its life.

### The icons

The twenty-seven icons are the items as a player knows them: the sixteen
weapons, base and evolved, the ten passives, and lamp oil. Each is told from
every other at a glance, and an evolved weapon's icon reads as a transformed
version of its base's. The HUD's slots and the level-up and chest overlays draw
them beside the names `specs/ui.md` states.

## Loading a produced file

Every produced file is loaded page-relative: the built site is served from a
sub-path as well as from a root, and the project's Vite config already emits
relative URLs.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Commit the files under `assets/`, import each PNG and `.wav` through the
bundler (or resolve it with `new URL(..., import.meta.url)`), and use the URL
that comes back.
{{else}}
Commit the files under `public/assets/`, which Vite serves at the engine's
asset root `assets/` and copies into `dist/` unchanged, and load each image
with `api.assets.loadImage` and each sound with `api.audio.load`, awaited in
`initialize`, so every asset is decoded before the first frame; the engine's
`assets.md` states the root and the path rules.
{{/if}}

Hold the decoded images and sounds in a module-level table filled once at
load and read by rendering and the audio path. The game's state carries data
alone, and the simulation reads no image.

## The sound

Produce a distinct sound for every cue `specs/ui.md` lists, with `sfx-synth`
and/or `sfx-sample` as suits each: `hit`, `kill`, `gem`, `hurt`, `level-up`,
`choose`, `chest`, `evolve`, `pickup`, `fallen`, `dawn`, `menu-move`,
`menu-confirm`, and the `hum` that loops under Halo and Corona. Produce the
`music` bed with `music`: a piece for the whole of a ten-minute night. `hum`
and `music` are each authored to loop cleanly, the file's end running into its
start with no click, no gap, and no jump in level.

Play the `.wav`s keyed to the cues exactly as `specs/ui.md` states, through
the runtime's audio path.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Decode each file with the Web Audio API and loop `music` and `hum` through a
looping buffer source.
{{else}}
Bind each cue to its file with `api.audio.load` and loop `music` and `hum`
through `api.audio.loop` and `api.audio.stop`.
{{/if}}

Sound reads the night's character, so hold it to the same bar as the sprites.
`hit` and `gem` sound many times a second late in the night, so each is short
and light; `kill` sits above `hit`; `hurt` is unmistakable in a crowd of the
other cues; `fallen` and `dawn` carry weight; `hum` sits under the music; and
the music bed stays welcome for the full ten minutes.

## What is drawn in code

Everything else on screen is drawn in code, with no produced file behind it:
the HUD's bars, pips, clock, kill count, cooldown readout, and slot frames;
every menu, overlay, and screen and all of their text; the ground, as the
produced tile repeated in world space; and the debug overlay. The produced
sprites sit in that scene; the scene itself is the build's.

## Genuinely produced

The assets are produced with these tools and wired into the game. Every moth,
bolt, and gem on screen is a produced sprite, every enemy visibly walks on its
produced frames, every slot and offer carries a produced icon, and every cue
plays a produced sound. Everything the game shows and plays traces to a file
produced here or to the chrome drawn in code listed above.
